      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-06-25_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>A'S MINT SELF-RAISING</head>
        <p>A'S MINT SELF-RAISING</p>
        <p>"CAYITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>In the early days of her Nlsjeety'm reign slmoet all occupations, save the Anny, Navy, Church end Barovere regarded as below the dignity of a w.. 11- horn gentleman. Bulwer Lytton wail perhaps the first aristocrat to break the barrier fie regarded writing for periodicals, and he took the editorship of a magazine with the avowed object of showing that such an occupation was not inconsietent with the position of a gentleman. To-day we have • diike'e non whoee name is advertised prominently an editor of a magazine, although no peers none have yet been found to brave the task of editing a London daily. Than we have great nobles, with their names on their carte, directly supplying the public with coals, and a noble earl has established a fruit and vegetable ehop hard by his great mansion near Chariag-croes. As for aristocratic lady milliners, they may be found by the dozen, whiist a gentleman of encient family, who is married to • peers daughter, is a sort of estrepey,i, for making all the arrangnmente for balls and parties. Then again up to 20 years age it wee considered • shuddering impossibility for the " upper clam*" to travel second elms, wherea, now many who mix in the hest society un- blushingly go third.</p>
        <p>--- Among the matters decided upon by my pre- deceseors at the Colonial office," to one Mr. Cham- berlain's phrase, was the cession of Swaziland to the Boers. What will the right hon. gentleman say When he hears that in the territory now beyond hie control a Transvaal official has jest discovered "no less than 30 new varieties of orchids which have never yet been culrbrated '? One of these "pos- sesses a bulb ecented like eau de cologne, and the largest flower even seen amongst South African orchids." If Mr. Chamberlain would only nettle up with Pretoria.. .Dm Paul might give him the free run in thin orchid l'aradixe.</p>
        <p>The great day Ime ;m—e—, —and, I rejoice to write, has gone, and it must be pronounced a complete success. They say that the Englieh du not know how to ergenion proceseion. That slander is now effectually dispelled. Nothing could be more is- pinking than tico magnificent parade of the Imperial ore..s, typical of the world-spreading range of leoglsnils power. within which drove in State the Queen and Empress amid eurroundings eloquent of triumphs greater than those achieved by any con- quering monarch of ancient days or modern. Nor was the procwsion it Pelf the most significant part of Tuesday's spectacle, for, striking un.l brilliant as; it was, still more wonderful is the fact that her Majesty drove for six long miles amid the serried ranks of crowding subjects. ranged tier on tier frcce pavement to roof, accompanied by one unceasing roar of &amp;edam. Impreesive Later pageant Waim t spectators along the line of route, this long con- tinued and ceaseless greeting, rolling along for mile ; after male, accompanied by the English blase of ulcers and far waving hats and thating hrindker. chiefs, must to Her Majesty have been something overwhelming. For an outward and visible climax of eixty yearn al sovereignty nothing could be more majeatic and soul-stirring. The British people on Tuesday wiped out the nig-; gardlinew of the Admiralty, and the sting of the early neglect of the Colonial Office. by the magni- flcient reception they gals to the Colonial escort. Every man of the escort and every Colonial visitor is rnerwhelmed with the enthusiasm of the wel- come given to them. The Colonial Premiers can hardly and words in which to express their genes of the warmth of the welcome accorded to the repre- sentative.; of the Colonlw. It was no stiff and studied ceremonial reception. but a ver'tahle out- pouring ot the feelings of the people. Whatever irapricsion thered-tapeism of the Government De- partments may haoe left upon the minif of 01.11 r ,INitOrtipthNV must have been made to feel that in the womation of the 1.11.11 here they are parts with °newlyrs of the great fabric of the Empire. The cheers that greeted them from the millions of the Engliith populace on Tuesday. and wherever else they have appeared, is a factor of unilieation of which the Imperial Federation League never dreamed.</p>
        <p>It will evidently take some time for London to settle down into its usual condition of prosaic workaday life. It has (mown absolutely 110 dis- position to burry beck to toil, Wednesday having been almost as completely given up to sightseeing at Timidity and Monday. Although some beginning hen been made with the removal of stands the deco- rations have for tie roost part remained untouched, and there has been nitty general renewal of illu- minations, with their attendant throngs. In some respects, indeed, Wednesdsy pulsated with crowded life almost more than Tuividay, for there is no doubt that myriads of people kept away through the alarming prognoeticstione that 1144 been iniele of the inconvenience of trani•it and the dangers of the streets. The result wail wen in the marked all-1 space of undue crowding, and on Wednesday one met everywhere with sagacious people who.refusing to share in the panicky booking of sesta before- hand,quiet ly went on foot to POMO point of vantage fI VA had an exceedingly good view of the prcression for nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-06-25_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
