        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>We sha:l no doubt enon turn from the meterirelogleta to what extent the prteent month of dune lA. been I phenomenal in its weather variatinisr. To the ordinary ,nerver who w dependent up m pers,nal ro .oilection would arem to be well-nigh with .ut precedent in Its pee cidiar and abnormal inartinstations. Bright and warm I mnahine, damp and cold air, and strong gales uctiene ponied by long-continued and heavy rains, with even showers of hail, have succeeded each other in less than • fortnight, and at • period of the year when we have unquestionably entered into oummer, and roue are usually blowing everywhere. But,ilepreuing as our ex- ps.renee has thus been in England, it ia trifling as com- pared with the cycloae which has surged the western suburbs of l'arie. Rarely in thee- latitudes ho norm so ternbill th it. force beret oars • popams diatnct. Manifestations of the kind are not uncommon on the American Gmtinent they am, honorer, very little knowa in Europe. 1 hey lottuately last ,nly a few moments, and sabeide a• mysteriously as they arms. acmcrding bi oheermition on the spot it wau,a.m usual, a urtow column of air moving with enormous velocity, coming from the west and deecnbing • parabolic line. Several villages which, from their proximity to the capitol, are largely populated by bulness Parisians, were struck an route and half levelled to the ground. Trees were up. rooted in hundreds, most of the bourn damaged mere or leas menously, the telegraph system doetrored, and, 'worst of allonany persons killed and injured. The dua- -1 tion of this Mast wee only about ten seconds, hut that sufficed to work imniense hay ic and Peri. is hue more called upon to give leoll. It. abundance for the succour of the stricken.</p>
