      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-06-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>hale figure in American public life. He 4 a leading politiciis in the Staten and a inillionaire te boot. Mr. Deenw has nut hitherto been a friendly critic of thin country, or d in fact he 1101f14, i,r at least held mail quite re, ently, the opinion that. war between this country and the United States is inevitable in the iroar future. Bat he has been impressed try Tuesday'li ptgealit in London. That procession, he says, has left a " lasting irnpres- Mon." " The concentrated and irropremsible joy and pride which preceded and accom- panied and followed the QUIEN like a resist. IPSS torrent surpassed anything ever witnessed before. Though many races and many tongues participated the dominant and absorbing expres- sion wee English, and the glory wag England's. Peers and cetnmoners, meaters and workmen, millionaires and the multitude wore welded by the treminelmis force of this concentration of loy- elty from the rmnotest corners tf the earth into one wild and frantic roam of patriotic enthus- iasm." And thee this eloquent American sums up by saying that thi. celebration " was a mighty poian to the victories of civil and religious liberty, the advancement of art, science and learning, the acquisition and settlement of new territories, the eetabliehment of law, infer and justice among barbarous people, and the peaceful progress and miraculous development of intellectual, moral and material power by liberty invention, steam and electricity, ill the el xty ye.irs of V noronia's In' dealing with the program and power of the Empirea good many panegyrical flights have been taken 1,3/ complacent ftritishere but 1101. OF them , have, excelled the rather gorgeous rhetoric ! of Mr. Der.. It is evidout therefore that the Jubilee celebration have, in addition to the merely specteculer effects, answered mime other very useful purposes. They have 1411.1 to strengthen the bonds which unite the Colonies with the Mother Country, and they have impromed visitors from abroad with the power of the Empire.</p>
        <p>A writer in the "Daily Chronicle" j neth" gives some information with regard l Legislatios. to the conditions of labour sixty ; years ago which shows that real progress has been made in this respect during the Ql7 reign. When the guess ascended the throne children of live mid six years of age were employed all the hours of the day and sometimes a largo part of the night in the factories of Lancashire and Other parts of the country. Those children were veritable slaves. In order to keep them to their tasks they were beaten and kicked by overseers in the °melba manner. One little tileotch girl told tho Commissioner licw when she was too small to put on her" via claithee" the overseer used loather fo till she screamed. The boys were beaten black and Woo with rope-onds, and fell to the floor crying out for mercy. The little piecors at Leices- ter worked from half-pest five in the morning " till : the dark him," lc the carpet manufacturing dO- tricts, the children were often celled up at three or four in the morning, and kppt on for fourteen or sixteen hones. One of them was found in the middle of a thirty-foor hours spell. The least chit- dren,who could not ho awakened at four or five in the morning were carried ta the mills on the hacks of the older children. But the mines were worse than the factories. In 1842 Government Cocoons- 'donors found pit workings swarming with miser- able children, sinne of them as young as four or Thew little things were found to be useful in dragging coal through ways which were only twenty-two inches high. Women and girls were employed in the mines to drag tubs over the rough ways and to do this they had to trawl Oa all fours. The arguments 140iinst factory legislation in those days were rely much the same as those hich have been used in later years. There was a Lord LON IlloN DISH&amp;T, as there is now, who was a great mine-owner and a resolute opponent of all improvements. tie declared Unit the collieries of Midlothian would have to he closed if the women wore shirt out, and hooves threatenol that if fac- tory inspectors went down lee pita they would not easily get out again. Great reforms have, how- ever, been affected ill spite of all the dismal pro- phecies, hut there is even yet room for improve- ment. The factory child of this country is still the youngest factory worker in Europe. •</p>
      </div>
