        <p>--- Ito Sunday, the membert of the 2nd Earl of Choder'• Volunteer Re..sment, enabled at the Drill Hall. Run- corn, and proceeded to the Parish Church where • thanksgiving service was held, in cornmanretion of the 130th annonernary I the Queen's arcane., There were on parade Captain H. F. Asians, Sergeant helmet°, Moyer. Mrs ser.eants, and 110 rank and file. At the ( hun•li tbe S ear, the Key. Con a Wood hand hie die- course on Proverbs viii 15, '' Be Mr kings reiyn " The o Volunteer band 'accompanied the hymns, and rendered the National Anthem during which the congregation I sad. At St P. 11.4 ChapeLtho Bev. T. Ilnekenbury preach. ling in the morning alluded to the Queen a.. daughter, awl mother, end also to the great inflara that she had wielded during her prolonged reign. Sullivan'. Jubilee hymn, and the National Anthem were sung. In the evening the choir rendered the ttfiollebijah Chats," and Gloria " from the 12th Mon. The Rev. W. Gage wan the preacher. At St. Alward's Catholic Church, leaven Lane, the expoeition of the Illeased Sacrament took place after the last masa. In the evening the Rosary was rated, and , Father Gra,, preached the wan. Be alluded to the record reign of Queen Victoria and contrasted the eon, ditios ot the Catholic Church noir, with that of SO years ego the annual Corpus Chriati proasion around the church, and the berealiction afterwards ',ink Oa. TUESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS, The people .1 Runcorn had evidently taken in anest the request of Mr. Taylor, chairman of the Han Coon. oil, to decorate their premisen. The arch over the street Itlet by the Primiti. Methodist schoolroom was exceed- • hasty pretty, and greet praise is due to those pereone who had taken no much pains in erecting it. Pieturn of Her majeaty were also in eyidenre,ne also were the mot- toes God Sive 011 T Al Am. On Tuesday On weather was excellent, and towards ton o'clock the streets began to gee crowded, and the different sorieties enabled at their headrpiartera to take part in the Jubilee procession. When all the pro- eessioniste had oasetnhled at the Market Square, 11PiPe- lions of music were given by the Foiled Bonds, The procession then moved off towards Church.etrert headed tor Superintendent Oltell.on horseback. Then followed the Runcorn Volunteer Band. and Mr. Samuel Tell's. J.P., C C.. chairman of the District Council,wearing the gold chain and badge nf office,preoen"ed to the town by Sir J. 1. Brunner. On either gide of Mr. Taylor are the Vlcar.the Row. Canon Msitlond Wood, and the Rey Dr. Preston.and Mr. J W. Lightburn, vice-chairman of the Urban Colonel'. Then folinwed the Bonn. W. ft Stables, H. C. Moyle. it. R. Firth, Sch..levllrece,Gage, T. Breckenbury, ttidoe, *not Morrie Th.' Magistrate* folhwed, Mr. F. J. Norman, Mr. .1 Handler, and Mr. Frank Jnhnenn (deputy inagistratn. 'den) Thy mem- bers of the Trban District Council came next, the Rey. Dr. Gaeteldi, Mesa. E. 'melon, W. Hondley, D. his- brown. 6 Hewitt, J. JOB., M. Sweetrnan,J. Pritchard, J. Dean..). florrefiold.H. J.Grice,Mr. It Mardian (are- ren), Mr. W. Wilding 'surveyor), Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. J. D. Jones, Mr. W. Jones,and Mr. Percival (nuisance immoral. Next followed the CO•nnMn. Of the Poor, with it, W. Sutton Insistent napery Then enne the mernbera of the SAinol Board with Mr. Mom role.) and Meesrs. {lowa and Howard (Melton). 'the Library Committee ramp next followed by the merare of the Technical Interims ti Cmimittee. 'Floe medical prartitinnere of the town hollowed. Neat cane klareMounnn,fienkinenn,Satle, Frith, Haddock, Smote. Barker, 'Turnhull. Littler, R. Ostler. J. Pritchal, A. it. Norman, M. nnVinn, Tim- mine,Poonett, and Crags. The tire Miamin came next on their engine which had been taatefuliv decerated. Superintendent Wright, and Deputy Supt. Wright mete in charge. Then came the Friendly Societies, Runcorn Enteric" Independent Order of Oldfolloses.lll.- cheater. Unity. Runcorn's Perin Grand United Order of Oddlellows, The Loyal Ancient Order of Shepherd a. Shepherd's 'Tent Lodge. The Wootton Bond preceded the Rose of Sharon Grand United Order of o.ldiellows. Then come the Widnes Subscription Band followed by the Lily of the Valley Grand nap,' (Odor "of Odolfellows Neat came the Duke of Devonshire Lnd,:e. fallowe,l by the Runcorn Sons of Temperance, who were 'waded try Weston Wesleyan ll,not. Mr. G. Fl. Kirkham and Mr. W. Hearath, were the marehalle. After gootag along Churchvtat, the procewion wended its may on Water. lo.o.road, over Weed's, BridEP. along Lnaland-rnool, up Shav/Atrort, down Greenswor-road, down Iligh.street,up Iln.lge.street, and down Walt.n.rnarl. When the pro. cession reached the crane return Innn mad, In Soak, drew 'en at the Market Square mid the whole neembly joined in singing °God ma the THE AFTERNOON PROCFASION. 'flu' precession of Sunday and day meholors took place in the afternoon. All the•ChOde enrnmenred to SIOWIII,IIO in the Market Square at about 1 30 and timely after two o'clock the 18 schools, including over 5,000 children were congregabd in the soma. On the stare leading to the Market Hall WPM Mr. Samuel Tanlor, 21', C.c.. Mr. Intl.'', Mr. J. Dean, Mr. E. Irninn,Mr.M. Sweet- mon. Mr. H. J. Grier, Mr. G. Hewitt, Mr. Jame. Hand- ley, Mr. Frith, Mr, J. Pritchard. Mr. J. W. Lightburn, Mr. E. Marshal!. Mr. J. Wilding, the Re. Canon Wood, T. Brw.k.nnu,v. w Rae. Fig, and Dr. Paton. [he Widnes Suhorription Rand, then gave the .01d Hundred" and Hon National Anthem, the aseembly joining in the chorue. Mr. W. Handley con. doacted. PRF.SENTATION To MR. S. TAYLOR. Mr. Handley said it Wan now hit pleareabin duty to add to the day's nrocee lines, a ceremony which though having no piece on the printed programme. wee he ventured to say not the least important or sincere It was no mean honour to have been deputed •Pllting that united gathering of the teacher" and nholara of the whole of the Sunday Schools of Runrorn, to pre- sent Mr. Ramie' Taylor J.P ,C.C., chairman of thy 8.