      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-06-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>TRE OLD FOLKS TREAT.</head>
        <p>TRE OLD FOLKS TREAT.</p>
        <p>„ ...... The Melee celebrations in Bonbon were brought to a close on Plumley evening, when a triat arts given hi the 4.1 d folk, in the Public Hall,Kuneorn. The hall had linen most tastefully decorated with Saga arm mettnee appropriate to the celebratione. There were 170 poemne who net lawn foe tes,the tables fining presided neer by Mrs F. J. Norman. Mrs. E. Marshall. Mr, ti Cloward, Mrs. I. W•od,Mrs. Rowlands. Mr. J. R. Froth, Mrs. P. Leathwoml,Mrs. Frith, Jon, Men A. Leethwandoind the Mire, Owen, Bowyer, Taylor, Cronhy, end 'word After teethe mon ari.r. presented with t.ohn,e, and the women with tee. An •saell,mi programme had been arranged for the old folk.' smotemont rno items in- cluded ,-Bdhga, ho, by the Glee Party, Mr. Wrier' Cooke, Mir Johnson, Mires C. Taylor and Sirnkin, Ton Little Phrls,Mr. Hooch, Mine tiimkin and Mr. Morris, Mr Scott, Ten Little Bone, and hy Mr. Bran Ch. The com- mittees litho auperintended the arrancententa wore : Toe Comtnituro Menne I. Wood, W. Crosby. T. Bowyer, W. Shaw.Wendland,and M. Sweetrnan. Storme Meters. Fl. Hughes and 54. Sweetnian. Hell Committe, Moms. Waelland,Riley, Williams, Shaw.Brneld, Leach, flarow,and Littletrime Entertainment Committee, The Mires Sheltie, E li. Wright, A. M. 'Taylor, Johnson,and Baker,and Messrs. WE. Lanerter,ll. Branch, Flooth,and W. Cooke. Scrutiny Committee Merre. 11,wyer,W11- limn% li. Cloward, Leattiwond, Sweetman, and en,lt. Cabe Committer Wren. J. Nall. S Littler, 0 Little. Parer and oilnr Mr.W. Millington,and Cloward acted as hon. reentries.</p>
        <p>A special commenieratisn service was held in the chapel on Monday mdrnlng, Juno 20. h. The rim .4 prayer used we. therms presented for all churehee on that det. Several jubilee hymn. were well rendered by the choir. The magnificent hymenll King of King. " written by the Bishop of Wakefield with mush- by Sir Arthur Sullivan,formed a lending lectern ot the service A very appropriate sermon era. preached hy the chap- lain, the Bev. L. It Lynn Kennett. —A Noncmformlat service was held in the afternoon when the hymen. were repeated and sn address We. given by Mr. Kay, of Kingsley. Eseh Nervier Was non-laded by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Jubilee Day the il',ll4P presented quite a lora! land (native appearance with its large Union Jack II W- ing gaily from a Itg dad high over the front entrance. Ey the kindne...f the livardiana, Chrietrima farm vras provided fur the inmate.. The usual breakfaat, consist- ing of milk, porridge, tea, bread and butter, was weed out at lam Dinner at noon •1•11 preceded by the sing- ing of the National Anthem and choert for the Ude,. The meal sou competed faplenfiful cup. ply of root beef and new potatoen, and WM followed by a diatribution of apples and oranges. Each arhalt inmate was supplied with a pint of ale, and each child with a bottle of aerated water Thie Irmo which was appreciated by those present, wan the gift of Mr .S. Linn., of Moore. Before separating a scene of great enthusiasm ensued,tho inmate. rim', and !amity cheering the Guardia., the Muter, Matron, and the other officer, of the inetitution. Supper which in. elided tee, eoffee, -read and butter, currant and nest cake, and stowed rhubarb, was partaken at 5 p en. The experiment of *Dewing the inmate. to help !hombres from the visnle placed before thorn wan tried for the Bent time and Was attended with interne, no ap reciable *Jute or abume of the privilege being noticed During the day the children were regaled with rherrins, note of kinda, and meets, whilot the smokers ermined fully to enjoy the extra ounce of tobacco provided for thorn. The youngetera wore also given mementoes of the occasion Intim form of medal., tho kind eirt. of Mr. T. Wright of 11.1tna, end Mr. S. Taylor 1 l'., (7.C., of Runcorn, the latter of whom alto inelnded some with diatinetion ribbon for the officers. It was pleaeng to notice the glea.e nf pride and matisfartion on the y ive. nilee fan. sa the medals were fastenci to their breasts The fruit catering which was left in the hand. of Mrs Massey of Acton Grange left nothing to he Melina&amp; When the "retiring boll rang general satiafactinn wan nwpre•sed at the thmmughly